{"name":"Echinoderm","hex":"#ffa565","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"echinoderm","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An animal of the phylum Echinodermata, comprising radially symmetric, spiny-skinned marine animals including seastars, sea urchins, sea cucumbers, crinoids, and sand dollars. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/echinoderm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Echinoderm","blocks":[{"text":"An echinoderm is any animal of the phylum Echinodermata, which includes starfish, brittle stars, sea urchins, sand dollars and sea cucumbers, as well as the sessile sea lilies or \"stone lilies\". While bilaterally symmetrical as larvae, as adults echinoderms are recognisable by their usually five-pointed radial symmetry, and are found on the sea bed at every ocean depth from the intertidal zone to the abyssal zone. The phylum contains about 7,600 living species, making it the second-largest group of deuterostomes after the chordates, as well as the largest marine-only phylum. The first definitive echinoderms appeared near the start of the Cambrian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Echinoderm_collage_2.jpg/330px-Echinoderm_collage_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Echinoderm","wikidata":"Q44631"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg/250px-Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg/500px-Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg/250px-Ophiolepis_paucispina_389644336.jpg?utm_sou